---
/**
 * Privacy. A paper page on the blog's own 44rem reading column, so the
 * policy is set like a post rather than like a legal notice.
 *
 * Reached from the nav drawer on every page as the last link, and from the
 * Footer's print line on the blog pages (which is also the no-JS way in,
 * since the drawer's toggle is hidden without JS).
 *
 * Everything on it is a fact about how the site is built, so it only needs
 * rewriting when the build changes: a form, an analytics script or a
 * third-party font would each make it wrong.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/+$/, '');
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy"
  description="What this site collects, which is nothing, and the two places data touches anything anyway."
  light
>
  <article>
    <header class="pt-32 pb-10 sm:pt-40">
      <div class="post-col mx-auto px-5 sm:px-8">
        <p>
          <a class="back" href={`${base}/`}>
            <span class="back-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&larr;</span>
            <span class="back-label type-label">Home</span>
          </a>
        </p>

        <h1 class="type-display mt-10 text-[clamp(2rem,5vw,3.5rem)]">Privacy</h1>

        <hr class="post-rule" />
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="pb-24 sm:pb-32">
      <div class="post-body post-col mx-auto px-5 sm:px-8">
        <p>
          This site collects nothing. There is no account to make, no analytics script, and no
          cookie. The one form is a password field on a single page, and it is checked inside your
          browser: what you type there is never sent anywhere. Nobody on the team can tell that you
          were here.
        </p>

        <p>
          That is less a policy than a description of how the site is built. It is a stack of static
          files, and every font it uses is served from the same place the pages are, so opening one
          does not hand your address to a type foundry or an ad network.
        </p>

        <p>
          The host is the exception, and only in the way every host is one. The site sits on GitHub
          Pages, whose servers record the requests they answer: an IP address, the page asked for,
          the time. We never see those logs and cannot query them. What GitHub does with them is
          covered by <a
            href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener">GitHub's own privacy statement</a
          >.
        </p>

        <p>
          The <a href={`${base}/drivetrain/`}>drivetrain calculator</a> writes your last configuration
          to your browser's local storage so the numbers are still there when you come back. That stays
          on your machine. Clearing site data for this domain deletes it.
        </p>

        <p>
          Links to Instagram and YouTube are ordinary links. Follow one and you are on their site
          under their rules, and neither of them tells us who arrived.
        </p>

        <p>
          Email us and we have your email address. Those messages sit in the team's Gmail and we use
          them to write back. Nobody gets added to a list.
        </p>

        <p>
          The roster shows students' names and photographs, published with their permission. If you
          are on it and want your photo or your name gone, write to <a
            href="mailto:gnceonyx@gmail.com">gnceonyx@gmail.com</a
          >. It comes down on the next deploy and you do not have to give a reason.
        </p>

        <p>Anything else about this page goes to the same address.</p>

        <p class="type-label text-faint">Last updated September 2026</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* The column, the rule and the back link all come from global.css: this
     page is the blog post with a policy in it, and the two share one
     declaration so they cannot drift apart. Only the closing dateline is
     this page's own. */

  /* The dateline that closes the page sits off the body's own rhythm. */
  .post-body .type-label {
    margin-top: 2.5em;
  }
</style>
